# preview_routes.caddy — the URL surface of the signed-in preview host.
#
# Imported by the preview.marinesensitivity.org vhost in the Caddyfile AND by
# caddy/test/preview_routes.test.Caddyfile (a throwaway, HTTP-only caddy with a
# TEST HS256 jwtauth in place of Cloudflare's JWKS), so what caddy/test/run.sh
# proves is exactly what production runs. Everything here executes AFTER the
# vhost's jwtauth: an unauthenticated request never reaches these routes.
#
# THE VERSION IS THE PATH. Cloudflare Access scopes its per-version reviewer
# policies by hostname + path (never by query string), so a restricted release
# lives at /{ver}/scores/, /{ver}/species/ and /docs/{ver}/ -- one Access
# application per version (server/cloudflare/access.sh) -- and never at
# /scores/?ver=. The path prefix is stripped here and `ver` is FORCED into the
# query for the app (msens::atlas_resolve_ver on the preview instance); the app
# then binds the session to that version with a signed token (msens::ver_token_*),
# because everything the client's JavaScript sends over the websocket is
# forgeable and a reviewer allowed on v9 must not be able to steer the shared
# preview process to v10.

# a reviewer's page must never be indexed
header X-Robots-Tag "noindex, nofollow"

# --- the apps: /{ver}/{app}/... -> rstudio:3839 /{app}/...?ver={ver} -------
# THE VERSION IS THE PATH, AND IT REACHES THE APP AS A HEADER -- not as ?ver=.
#
# shiny-server serves the app at /scores, so the /{ver}/ prefix is stripped here
# and the version travels in `X-MS-Version`, which Caddy SETS from the path (a
# client cannot forge it, exactly like X-MS-User). ui(req) reads it; the public
# host, which has no prefix, keeps using ?ver=.
#
# This replaces forcing `uri query ver` onto the request, which was wrong twice
# over. Practically: shiny-server's client bundle at `<app>/__assets__/...` comes
# from a handler that 404s if the request carries ANY query string, so forcing
# `ver` there 404'd shiny-server-client.min.js and left the app hanging on
# "Loading map..." (preShinyInit undefined, then a raw `wss://.../websocket/`
# shiny-server never answers). Conceptually: a query the server writes into a URL
# the user then sees and can edit is a worse channel than a header the server
# controls -- and it left `?ver=v8` duplicating the `/v8/` already in the path of
# every link a reviewer might share. Dropping it also collapses what were two
# route blocks (page vs subresource) back into one.
import app_version_routes.caddy rstudio:3839

# --- an UNVERSIONED app path on the preview host is never proxied ----------
# It would sit outside every per-version Access policy (any signed-in reviewer
# could reach any restricted release), so it must not reach shiny-server. Send
# the reviewer to the landing page (the version list) rather than the 404 the
# landing file_server would answer. The ?ver= spellings above match first.
@oldapp path /scores /scores/* /species /species/*
redir @oldapp / 302

# --- restricted docs: /docs/{ver}/... -> the gh-pages-preview clone ---------
handle_path /docs/* {
	root * /share/docs_preview
	file_server
}

# --- landing page: lists the restricted releases from versions.json --------
handle {
	root * /share/github/MarineSensitivity/server/caddy/preview
	file_server
}
